    "narrative": "The centerpiece of today\u2019s offensive strategy is **Lawrence Butler**, whose score of 74.61 makes him our strongest play against Walbert Ure\u00f1a. His matchup is the ideal confluence of a favorable 4.68 xERA opposing starter and a +10% park factor, bolstered by significant platoon advantages. **Daulton Varsho** follows closely at 66.12; his upside is maximized by the soft matchup against Rea and a robust +13% SP skill bonus, which offsets the caution regarding Rea\u2019s hold percentage. Similarly, **Bryson Stott** (63.92) is a mandatory start. While facing a tough arm in Freddy Peralta, the combination of a favorable wind and the specific strategic nuance of Peralta\u2019s slow delivery\u2014which increases the probability of a stolen base\u2014makes **Bryson Stott** a high-leverage choice in our pursuit of the scarcest category.\n\nThe most difficult lineup decision today involved **Jonathan Aranda**, who narrowly edged out the bench options with a 55.67. While **Jeremiah Jackson** (40.83) and **Drew Gilbert** (40.95) are flirting with the \"below floor\" threshold due to brutal matchups against Yoshinobu Yamamoto and the Meyer/Marlins pairing respectively, we are forced to lean on their volume to maintain our caps. **Jeremiah Jackson** in particular remains a risk; the -17% SP skill penalty against Yamamoto is punishing, and his floor is only salvaged by favorable wind conditions. We are also closely monitoring the pending status of **Cal Raleigh**, **Geraldo Perdomo**, **Jeremiah Jackson**, **Lawrence Butler**, and **Taylor Ward**. Should their projected spots in the batting order shift downward, we may see further degradation in their efficiency scores. With **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, **Will Smith**, and **Dylan Beavers** sidelined on the IL, our roster depth is tested, leaving us with minimal flexibility should these late-afternoon lineups trigger negative adjustments.",
